<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vakataka dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 250–500</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Vākāṭaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vakataka dynasty was an ancient Indian dynasty that arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in the mid-3rd century CE and became the most important successor of the Satavahanas there, contemporary with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> in the north. Little is known of its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhyashakti">Vindhyashakti</a> (r. 250–270); expansion began under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i">Pravarasena I</a>, after whom the family is thought to have divided into branches, two of them known. The Gupta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a> married his daughter into the dynasty. The Vakatakas were patrons of art and literature: the Buddhist caves of Ajanta were built under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena-vakataka">Harishena</a> (c. 475–500). The end of the dynasty is unknown, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> later filled the vacuum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty.jpg" alt="Vakataka dynasty" width="330" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_Padmapani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
